{"name":"Emanation","hex":"#b4a3bb","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"emanation","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of flowing or proceeding (of something, quality, or feeling) from a source or origin.","That which issues, flows, or proceeds from any object as a source; efflux; an effluence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/emanation"}],"entries":[{"title":"Radon","blocks":[{"text":"Radon is a chemical element; it has symbol Rn and atomic number 86. It is a radioactive noble gas and is colorless and odorless. Of the three naturally occurring radon isotopes, only 222Rn has a sufficiently long half-life for it to be released from the soil and rock where it is generated. Radon isotopes are the immediate decay products of radium isotopes."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/be/Radon86%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radon"},{"title":"Emanation (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Emanation is a live album by the Sam Rivers Trio, led by multi-instrumentalist and composer Rivers, and featuring double bassist Cecil McBee and drummer Norman Connors. It was recorded on June 3, 1971, at the Jazz Workshop in Boston, and was released in 2019 by NoBusiness Records as volume 1 of the Sam Rivers Archive Series."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/90/Sam_Rivers_Emanation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emanation_(album)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/be/Radon86%281%29.jpg/250px-Radon86%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/be/Radon86%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/be/Radon86%281%29.jpg/250px-Radon86%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadon86(1).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Radon_spectrum.png/500px-Radon_spectrum.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Radon_spectrum.png/1280px-Radon_spectrum.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Radon_spectrum.png/500px-Radon_spectrum.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Emission spectrum of radon-222 (radium emanation), photographed by Ernest Rutherford in 1908. Numbers at the side of the spectrum are wavelengths. The middle spectrum is of radon-222, while the outer two are of helium (added to calibrate the wavelengths).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadon_spectrum.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Radon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg/500px-Radon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Radon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg/1280px-Radon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Radon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg/500px-Radon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Radon in a cloud chamber. The ionizing radiation of radon causes condensation to appear as cloud tracks in the chamber.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Radon_apparatus.png/330px-Radon_apparatus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Radon_apparatus.png/330px-Radon_apparatus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Apparatus used by Ramsay and Whytlaw-Gray to isolate radon. M is a capillary tube, where approximately 0.1 mm3 were isolated. Radon mixed with hydrogen entered the evacuated system through siphon A; mercury is shown in black.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadon_apparatus.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Lead210inairatjapan.png/500px-Lead210inairatjapan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Lead210inairatjapan.png/500px-Lead210inairatjapan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"210Pb is formed from the decay of 222Rn. Here is a typical deposition rate of 210Pb as observed in Japan as a function of time, due to variations in radon concentration.[67]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALead210inairatjapan.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Radon_Concentration_next_to_Uranium_Mine.PNG/500px-Radon_Concentration_next_to_Uranium_Mine.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Radon_Concentration_next_to_Uranium_Mine.PNG/500px-Radon_Concentration_next_to_Uranium_Mine.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Radon concentration next to a uranium mine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadon_Concentration_next_to_Uranium_Mine.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Radon_Lognormal_distribution.gif/500px-Radon_Lognormal_distribution.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campa